00:06:38.200 --> 00:06:41.840
without being really obsessive, about 100% test coverage,

123
00:06:41.840 --> 00:06:45.000
which I think is not really the right kind of aim.

124
00:06:46.200 --> 00:06:48.480
The great thing about

125
00:06:48.480 --> 00:06:52.240
copying an existing system like S3 is you've got this test oracle.

126
00:06:52.240 --> 00:06:54.120
You can find out what happens when you do something.

127
00:06:54.120 --> 00:06:59.240
You run the test, you can run it against the you can run it in my case against S3,

128
00:06:59.280 --> 00:07:04.600
and I have 1500 tests that run against S3 and they lock down the behavior.

129
00:07:04.760 --> 00:07:08.360
Then you tell the AI you have to do it exactly like that,

130
00:07:08.360 --> 00:07:11.680
and it's much better at doing things when it does that,

131
00:07:11.880 --> 00:07:16.760
you know, it basically gives it a nice baseline.

132
00:07:16.880 --> 00:07:21.880
It's so much so that I actually think that if you're writing a complex system,

133
00:07:22.120 --> 00:07:23.920
the one way to start is to write

134
00:07:23.920 --> 00:07:26.920
a really, really simple version that's kind of trivial,

135
00:07:26.960 --> 00:07:31.480
that basically has the same behavior that you can use as this test oracle, so

136
00:07:31.480 --> 00:07:34.680
that when you're building a more complex version, you don't make it break.

137
00:07:34.840 --> 00:07:40.720
And you can build a test suite against a simple version first.

138
00:07:42.040 --> 00:07:44.280
It's not 100%

139
00:07:44.280 --> 00:07:49.280
easy like you pointed at Amazon S3, for example, and you discover

140
00:07:49.680 --> 00:07:54.320
all sorts of kind of weird things like S3.

141
00:07:54.400 --> 00:07:59.640
Authorization is a lot of it's eventually consistent, not immediate.

142
00:07:59.640 --> 00:08:02.640
So you have to keep working out well.

143
00:08:02.640 --> 00:08:04.800
We need a load of retries in this test.

144
00:08:04.800 --> 00:08:08.200
Otherwise it'll it looks like it's a the behavior

145
00:08:08.200 --> 00:08:11.200
is different and things like that.

146
00:08:11.440 --> 00:08:14.640
And so you have to kind of do some interpretation

147
00:08:14.680 --> 00:08:18.800
even with a kind of exact text oracle which is kind of annoying.

148
00:08:19.000 --> 00:08:22.920
So it's not it's not quite a specification,

149
00:08:23.000 --> 00:08:28.200
but it kind of does keep you very, very grounded, which is really important.

150
00:08:28.560 --> 00:08:33.440
The other thing about having a test oracle of something you're trying to copy,

151
00:08:33.640 --> 00:08:37.200
whether it's an existing system of yours or someone else's, is that

152
00:08:37.720 --> 00:08:40.720
the public API doesn't cover behavior

153
00:08:40.720 --> 00:08:43.720
you can't really see that goes on in the background.

154
00:08:43.760 --> 00:08:47.400
And so you can't tell

155
00:08:47.400 --> 00:08:51.640
when things happen or invisible behaviors about things.

156
00:08:51.640 --> 00:08:56.240
You have to kind of either infer them from something you can observe, or

157
00:08:57.400 --> 00:09:00.760
you need to find some other way of testing those kind of behaviors.

158
00:09:00.760 --> 00:09:03.760
So you can't see

159
00:09:03.800 --> 00:09:06.920
when an item really gets deleted in S3.

160
00:09:06.920 --> 00:09:10.560
It kind of happens at some point.

161
00:09:10.560 --> 00:09:13.600
But you know, you there's no API that lets

162
00:09:13.600 --> 00:09:16.600
you see behind the scenes of that.

163
00:09:16.600 --> 00:09:19.080
The other thing,

164
00:09:19.080 --> 00:09:22.920
like you think S3 is really nicely documented.

165
00:09:22.960 --> 00:09:25.720
It's got all these hundreds of pages of documentation.

166
00:09:25.720 --> 00:09:28.240
The documentation turns out to mostly be wrong.

167
00:09:28.240 --> 00:09:31.520
And every single detail when you actually look at the details.

168
00:09:31.560 --> 00:09:35.320
So again, having the test oracle and running

169
00:09:35.320 --> 00:09:39.080
the test is much more important than reading the documentation.

170
00:09:40.600 --> 00:09:42.960
If you I think the documentation, you know,

171
00:09:42.960 --> 00:09:46.760
maybe it was true once, maybe it was, maybe it's approximately true.

172
00:09:46.760 --> 00:09:50.400
It kind of gives you hints about things that are interesting, but

173
00:09:50.400 --> 00:09:53.480
never trust anyone's documentation at all.

174
00:09:53.680 --> 00:09:56.640
But again, that gives you grounded tests

175
00:09:56.640 --> 00:10:00.520
on what real behavior is and real edge cases.

176
00:10:02.760 --> 00:10:05.760
Edge cases are really interesting

177
00:10:06.320 --> 00:10:10.800
because they kind of it was it was kind of a while.

178
00:10:10.880 --> 00:10:15.360
And when I found when I was actually working on

179
00:10:15.880 --> 00:10:21.720
improving the type system in the code, and the AI was writing

180
00:10:21.720 --> 00:10:25.520
some test cases for this random against S3 and found a 500 error,

181
00:10:25.520 --> 00:10:27.040
which was repeatable.

182
00:10:27.040 --> 00:10:32.160
And that was kind of quite exciting because this was,

183
00:10:32.200 --> 00:10:35.240
you know, this was an interesting edge case that had come up.

184
00:10:35.280 --> 00:10:39.080
It was implementing the code, and it just thought, well,

185
00:10:39.080 --> 00:10:43.160
I'll test this against S3 and see what happens.

186
00:10:43.160 --> 00:10:46.600
And clearly Amazon did not have a test case for this.

187
00:10:46.720 --> 00:10:47.560
And I did.

188
00:10:47.560 --> 00:10:51.480
And it actually gave me confidence that I was fine as soon as I found that

189
00:10:51.480 --> 00:10:56.120
I was like my test suites actually good now or parts of it are good now

190
00:10:56.120 --> 00:11:01.840
because I'm finding really weird errors and really weird edge cases.

191
00:11:01.880 --> 00:11:07.320
That means I must have kind of explored a lot of the of the universe of testing.

192
00:11:07.640 --> 00:11:11.320
And so it's kind of because it's kind of weird when you're doing this

193
00:11:11.360 --> 00:11:15.600
kind of complex development, because sometimes you feel everything's

194
00:11:15.600 --> 00:11:20.480
terrible and everything is really bad, and like, this is never going to work.

195
00:11:20.680 --> 00:11:23.560
And then other times you feel, oh, actually, yeah,

196
00:11:23.560 --> 00:11:24.960
this is kind of working again.

197
00:11:24.960 --> 00:11:27.440
So this was kind of

198
00:11:27.440 --> 00:11:30.800
the kind of thing about testing that gives you this kind of confidence.

199
00:11:30.800 --> 00:11:35.120
It found another one, another repeatable 500 error later

200
00:11:35.160 --> 00:11:36.360
in another weird edge case.

201
00:11:36.360 --> 00:11:41.360
And I was like, okay, how many of these there are any two so far?

202
00:11:42.880 --> 00:11:44.200
It was.

203
00:11:44.200 --> 00:11:48.680
So I was quite good at the edge cases,

204
00:11:48.680 --> 00:11:53.640
particularly kind of when it's actually writing, writing code itself.

205
00:11:53.680 --> 00:11:54.800
It was.

206
00:11:54.800 --> 00:11:57.840
I kept finding edge cases by reading the

207
00:11:57.840 --> 00:12:00.840
AWS documentation thinking.

208
00:12:00.880 --> 00:12:02.920
Is that really true?

209
00:12:02.920 --> 00:12:04.080
Have we got tests for this?

210
00:12:04.080 --> 00:12:08.920
Asking the AI to write tests for them, and finding other weird things that were

211
00:12:09.320 --> 00:12:14.360
approximately equal to the documentation or hints from the documentation.

212
00:12:14.360 --> 00:12:16.000
When I pointed the AI at the docs

